  "id": "oodham_traditional_song",
  "name": "O'odham traditional song (Tohono O'odham ceremonial and social song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song of the Tohono O'odham (formerly called Papago) and Akimel O'odham (Pima) of the Sonoran Desert, sung in cycles for the summer saguaro-wine rain ceremony, for curing, for the Wi:gita ceremony, and for social round and skipping dances. Singers accompany themselves with gourd rattles and by striking or scraping an overturned basket used as a drum, and a large Papago repertoire was recorded on cylinder in the early 1920s for Papago Music (1929). A hand-drum card stands in for the basket drum and a generic shaker card for the gourd rattles.",
